let __v_575 = pyrt::literals::decode("{\"$dict\":[[\"tortilla\",\"Plain flour, salt, a little oil or lard and warm water, nothing else: about 250g of flour to 150ml of warm water and a tablespoon of oil. Knead for two minutes, rest the dough under a bowl for 20, then roll each one out as thin as you can. Cook in a dry, very hot pan for about 30 seconds a side - they want speckles, not browning, or they go crisp instead of foldable. Press the bubble down with a spatula as it puffs and stack them under a tea towel.\"],[\"chapati\",\"Wholemeal flour and water, no raising agent and no yoghurt: about 200g of wholemeal or atta to 120ml of warm water, a pinch of salt and a smear of oil. Knead for five minutes until smooth, rest 20 minutes, roll thin, then cook in a dry pan for a minute a side until it speckles. Hold it over a flame with tongs for a couple of seconds at the end and it puffs up.\"],[\"pitta\",\"Pitta needs yeast, because the pocket is steam trapped inside the dough: 250g of strong white flour, 150ml of warm water, 5g of instant yeast, 5g of salt and a teaspoon of oil. Knead, prove an hour until doubled, then roll out about 1cm thick - too thin and there is no steam to open it. Bake on a tray as hot as the oven goes, 250C, for about five minutes, and they puff and hollow as they cool.\"],[\"quick\",\"Self-raising flour, a pinch of salt and enough yoghurt or water to make a soft dough: about 250g of flour to 150g of yoghurt, kneaded for a minute and rested for 10. Roll them thin, then cook each one in a dry, very hot frying pan for a minute a side until it blisters and puffs. Keep them under a tea towel while you do the rest, and eat them the same day; they freeze well between sheets of paper.\"]]}")?;
